# What git never tracks in c-rust-learning.
#
# WHY: this repository is public. Build output, core dumps, disk images and local
# configuration would publish noise at best and memory contents or secrets at worst.
# Every group below says why it is here. Cargo.lock is deliberately NOT ignored — see
# the Rust group.

# ── C build output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every exercise builds into its own build/ directory (code/docs/BUILD.md); the loose
# patterns catch a hand-run `gcc` outside make. *.d are the -MMD -MP dependency files.
*.o
*.a
*.so
*.d
a.out
build/
# A directory whose name ends in .d (a P6 rootfs overlay's etc/profile.d/, say) is
# source, not a dependency file — re-include directories only.
!*.d/

# ── Core dumps ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A core dump or valgrind's vgcore.<pid> is a copy of a crashed process's memory:
# large, machine-specific, and never something to publish. A directory called core/
# is source, so it is re-included.
core
core.[0-9]*
!core/
vgcore.*
# valgrind's profiling tools (callgrind, cachegrind, massif) write their output to the
# current folder, and each file records absolute source paths from this machine.
callgrind.out.*
cachegrind.out.*
massif.out.*

# ── Coverage output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# gcov data from a `--coverage` build; regenerated on every run.
*.gcda
*.gcno
*.gcov

# ── Rust ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cargo's build directory. Cargo.lock is committed, not ignored: the workspace builds
# binaries, and a committed lockfile makes `cargo test` resolve the same dependency
# set on every machine and in CI.
target/

# ── Kernel module build output (P4 onwards) ───────────────────────────────────
# kbuild writes these beside an out-of-tree module's source; all are rebuilt from it.
# Kernel source trees themselves are never committed (.claude/CLAUDE.md Section 5).
*.ko
*.mod
*.mod.c
.*.cmd
Module.symvers
modules.order

# ── Disk images and initramfs (P4 onwards) ────────────────────────────────────
# QEMU disk images, ISOs and initramfs archives are large build products, rebuilt
# from committed configs and scripts; the kernel safety rule keeps them out of git.
*.qcow2
*.img
*.iso
initramfs*.cpio*

# ── Editors and operating systems ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-person editor state and swap files; the shared settings are .editorconfig.
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# ── Environment files ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing in this repository needs a secret, so no environment file belongs in it.
# Ignored anyway: a public repository is the worst place to learn that lesson.
.env*

# ── Claude Code runtime ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# settings.local.json holds per-machine permission approvals; worktrees/ holds
# nested git checkouts; the lock file is runtime state. The shared, committed
# configuration is .claude/settings.json.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock
# CLAUDE.local.md holds one person's private instructions for this project, and Claude
# Code's memory documentation says to keep it out of version control. Ignored anywhere,
# since Claude Code loads one from any directory it works in.
CLAUDE.local.md

# code-review-graph's local database, if that tool is ever run here.
.code-review-graph/

# The manual is .claude/CLAUDE.md. Tools that generate a root CLAUDE.md (for example
# `code-review-graph install`) would otherwise add a second, drifting manual.
/CLAUDE.md
